{
  "claims": [
    {
      "claim": "The alarm system has a 100% guarantee that if there is an unauthorized access attempt (thief), it will trigger an SMS message.",
      "supported": true,
      "evidence_or_problem": "Source: [01:04:18\u201301:04:50] \"the produce of these anti-thief systems gives 100% guarantee that if there's any kind of unauthorized access attempt, I will definitely receive SMS message\""
    },
    {
      "claim": "False alarms occur in 10% of cases when there is a small earthquake (and no thief).",
      "supported": true,
      "evidence_or_problem": "Source: [01:04:50\u201301:05:21] \"it was in his manual guide that the system may produce false alarm because of small earthquakes. And false alarms are generated in 10% of the cases when there was a small earthquake.\""
    },
    {
      "claim": "The probability of a small earthquake (E=1) is 1/100 per day (1 per 100 days).",
      "supported": true,
      "evidence_or_problem": "Source: [01:08:20\u201301:08:55] \"in Bremen, small earthquakes happen on average once per 100 days\""
    },
    {
      "claim": "The probability of a thief (T=1) is 2/10,000 per day (2 robberies per 10,000 flats).",
      "supported": true,
      "evidence_or_problem": "Source: [01:08:20\u201301:08:55] \"on average, there's two rubber attempts per 10,000 of wax\" (likely typo for 'flats')"
    },
    {
      "claim": "The probability of a radio message (R=1) given a small earthquake (E=1) is 0.5.",
      "supported": true,
      "evidence_or_problem": "Source: [01:08:55\u201301:09:25] \"if there was small earthquake, the radio may decide to announce or may decide not to announce depending on what other news happened during that day\" (interpreted as 50% probability)"
    },
    {
      "claim": "The probability of a radio message (R=1) given no earthquake (E=0) is 0.",
      "supported": true,
      "evidence_or_problem": "Source: [01:08:55\u201301:09:25] \"if there was no earthquake, radio will not allow me\" (interpreted as 0 probability)"
    },
    {
      "claim": "The probability of the alarm (A=1) given a thief (T=1) and no earthquake (E=0) is 1.",
      "supported": true,
      "evidence_or_problem": "Source: [01:10:10\u201301:10:52] \"if there is a thief, the system will always work\""
    },
    {
      "claim": "The probability of the alarm (A=1) given no thief (T=0) and a small earthquake (E=1) is 0.1.",
      "supported": true,
      "evidence_or_problem": "Source: [01:10:10\u201301:10:52] \"false error, when there was an earthquake, no thief, the system will be turned on in 10% of the cases\""
    }
  ],
  "clarity_1_to_5": 4,
  "caveat": "The ASR has typos (e.g., 'rubber' for 'robberies', 'wax' for 'flats', 'ARM' for 'alarm'). The probability of R given E=1 is described as 'depending on what other news happened' which may imply uncertainty beyond 0.5. The lecture does not explicitly state that false alarms are conditional on no thief, though this is implied by context."
}
